# .gitattributes — settle line-ending handling on this repository.
#
# There was none before this file, and the checked-out Windows environment's
# core.autocrlf=true has no per-repo override to consult. The observed
# symptom: `apps/web/payload-types.ts` (a file Payload regenerates during a
# build) reported as modified with a blob byte-identical to HEAD -
# `docs/qa/2026-09-03-phase-1-closing-sweep.md`'s finding. Without this file,
# any generated or hand-edited text file is at the mercy of whatever
# core.autocrlf happens to be set to on the machine that touches it, which is
# not a property of this repository and should not decide what git considers
# "changed" in it.
#
# `text=auto` normalises every text file to LF in the repository itself,
# regardless of a contributor's own `core.autocrlf`, so the stored history is
# stable and a build tool that writes LF (as Payload's codegen does) cannot
# disagree with what git already has.
#
# `eol=lf` settles the other half, and it was missing for seventy-eight
# commits. `text=auto` alone says what the REPOSITORY stores; it says nothing
# about what a checkout WRITES, so on a machine with `core.autocrlf=true` -
# git's Windows default, and this project's own platform - a fresh clone
# materialises every text file with CRLF. `prettier --check .` defaults to
# `endOfLine: "lf"`, and `format:check` is a step of `npm run verify`, so
# `git clone` followed by README.md's own setup block reported "Code style
# issues found in 389 files" and exit 1. Measured on a clone with this
# repository's own prettier binary, by the seventh whole-branch review.
# CLAUDE.md §11 asks for a gate a developer can always pass honestly and
# CLAUDE.md §1.2 asks for copy-pasteable setup; both were false on Windows
# and true on the Linux CI runner, which is why seven rounds of gates on this
# branch never saw it. `eol=lf` makes the working tree match the repository on
# every platform, so the gate is the same gate everywhere it runs.
#
# Genuinely binary paths are marked explicitly so they are never run through a
# text filter at all - `-linux.png` is Playwright's visual-regression baseline
# set, and a filter-mangled byte in one of those would fail the visual suite in
# a way that reads as a design regression, not as a line-ending accident.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.woff2 binary
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
